Amazon states that its businesses encompass a large variety of product types, service offerings, and delivery channels, and that the worldwide marketplace in which it competes is evolving rapidly and is intensely competitive, with a broad array of competitors from many different industry sectors around the world [1] [2]. Its current and potential competitors include:
(1) physical, e-commerce, and omnichannel retailers, publishers, vendors, distributors, manufacturers, and producers of the products offered and sold to consumers and businesses [1] [2];
(2) publishers, producers, and distributors of physical, digital, and interactive media of all types and all distribution channels [1] [2];
(3) web search engines, comparison shopping websites, social networks, web portals, virtual assistants, and other online and app-based means of discovering, using, or acquiring goods and services, either directly or in collaboration with other retailers (including through artificial intelligence in FY2025) [1] [2];
(4) companies that provide e-commerce services, including website development and hosting, omnichannel sales, inventory and supply chain management, advertising, fulfillment, customer service, and payment processing [1] [2];
(5) companies that provide fulfillment and logistics services for themselves or for third parties, whether online or offline [1] [2];
(6) companies that provide information technology services or products, including on-premises or cloud-based infrastructure, tools and services relating to artificial intelligence, and other services [1] [2];
(7) companies that design, manufacture, market, or sell consumer electronics, communications, and other electronic devices and services [1] [2];
(8) companies that sell grocery products online and in physical stores [1] [2];
(9) companies that provide advertising services, whether in digital or other formats [1] [2]; and
(10) providers of virtual or in-person healthcare services [1] [2].
Amazon believes that the principal competitive factors in its retail businesses include selection, price, and convenience, including fast and reliable fulfillment, while additional competitive factors for its seller and enterprise services include the quality, speed, and reliability of its services and tools, as well as customers' ability and willingness to change business practices [1] [2]. Furthermore, some current and potential competitors have greater resources, longer histories, more customers, greater brand recognition, and greater control over inputs critical to various businesses [1] [2]. They may secure better terms from suppliers, adopt more aggressive pricing, pursue restrictive distribution agreements, direct consumers to their own offerings, lock-in potential customers with restrictive terms, and devote more resources to technology, infrastructure, fulfillment, and marketing [1] [2]. The internet and other technologies including artificial intelligence also facilitate competitive entry and comparison shopping, enhancing the ability of new, smaller, or lesser-known businesses to compete [1] [2].
